1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
A 45-story crane collapses in high winds.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Explosions kill hundreds in Chinese coal mines.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
A state-of-the-art dam bursts with catastrophic results.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
A tool for fitting shoes turns deadly.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Environmental disasters imperil wildlife in a California sea and devastate native peoples in Central Asia.

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Milwaukee, Wisconsin. An industrial city hard on the banks of Lake Michigan.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Wind and rain regularly sweep across the lake.

9
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:57,440
the lake so in the 1990s milwaukee decided to build a new weather resistant baseball stadium

10
00:00:57,441 --> 00:01:03,760
for the hometown brewers miller park would feature a unique fan-shaped retractable roof

11
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:11,840
a 12 000 ton giant costing 50 million dollars with seven panels that open or close in 10 minutes

12
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,880
installing the roof required one of the world's largest machines

13
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:20,280
the 45-story tall Big Blue Crane.

14
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:24,240
A crawler crane, Big Blue can travel over ground

15
00:01:24,241 --> 00:01:26,680
while simultaneously maneuvering its giant arm.

16
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:32,680
At 567 feet, it's nearly twice as tall as the Statue of Liberty.

17
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:35,340
The crane was huge.

18
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:38,220
It was far taller than the top of the stadium.

19
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:41,280
It was literally about 100 feet above the top of the stadium.

20
00:01:41,281 --> 00:01:52,220
On July 14, 1999, crane operators prepared to lift the single largest section of the roof,

21
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:55,700
a piece weighing an estimated 450 tons.

22
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:59,800
The load they were carrying was about three-eighths of an acre,

23
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:04,640
which is larger than the average house lot in a development.

24
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:09,880
The average house lot's got like 10,000 square feet in it, you know, a quarter of an acre.

25
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:14,820
And here we've got three-eighths or almost a half of an acre, you know, almost twice that.

26
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,140
Big blue cranes require four people to run them.

27
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:23,320
One operator sits in the front crawler unit, which supports the boom.

28
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:29,220
Another is in the rear crawler, which contains up to four million pounds of gravel that acts as ballast.

29
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:36,280
The third operator, who runs the lift cables, sits in a cab on the 100-foot-long truss that connects the two sections.

30
00:02:36,281 --> 00:02:43,040
all three men are largely blind to the cranes overall maneuvering so they follow radio

31
00:02:43,041 --> 00:02:52,860
instructions from a supervisor on the ground coming down now at 5 15 p.m with the roof piece

32
00:02:52,861 --> 00:02:59,240
suspended high above first base big blue began to buckle my friend and i looked at each other

33
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:02,260
and said that ain't right there's something wrong with this and then all of a sudden the

34
00:03:02,261 --> 00:03:04,320
just started drifting and we're like, are they moving?

35
00:03:04,321 --> 00:03:06,460
And then it's like, no, it's not moving.

36
00:03:06,461 --> 00:03:07,460
They're not moving it.

37
00:03:07,461 --> 00:03:08,940
It's coming on its own.

38
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:14,640
Oh, it was just an incredible loud sequence of noises.

39
00:03:14,641 --> 00:03:17,300
First, you heard the brakes coming off of the crane.

40
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:21,180
Then you heard the first pop.

41
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:24,520
Then you heard the next pop.

42
00:03:24,521 --> 00:03:25,520
The hell is it?

43
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,120
What's going on here?

44
00:03:28,121 --> 00:03:30,720
And then you heard a cacophony of sound

45
00:03:30,721 --> 00:03:32,720
where everything is coming down.

46
00:03:32,721 --> 00:03:34,720
Watch it, watch it!

47
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:50,720
I got it on.

48
00:03:50,721 --> 00:03:53,720
Afterwards, as I went to the inner part of the stadium,

49
00:03:53,721 --> 00:03:56,720
I saw what a huge mass of tangled steel and rubble

50
00:03:56,721 --> 00:03:59,720
that existed on the center part of the stadium.

51
00:03:59,721 --> 00:04:07,180
We had hundreds of tons of steel intermingled into the world's largest pickup sticks pile I have ever seen.

52
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:11,560
Amid the rubble lay the greatest cost of the disaster.

53
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:18,340
Chunks of concrete roof debris had struck a basket containing three steel workers,

54
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,440
sending them plummeting 300 feet to their deaths.

55
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:27,320
There were three people sitting near home plate, down near home plate.

56
00:04:27,321 --> 00:04:32,400
and that has one hell of an impact on people.

57
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:35,000
I still don't go to ballgames,

58
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:37,060
because if I look at home plate, I'll think of it.

59
00:04:37,061 --> 00:04:41,020
So I don't think I'll ever see a Miller Park ballgame.

60
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:50,460
The disaster stunned the citizens of Milwaukee

61
00:04:50,461 --> 00:04:52,560
and shook an entire industry.

62
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:56,760
For the crane industry, it was like 10 on the Richter scale.

63
00:04:56,761 --> 00:05:01,520
One of our icons was down, it collapsed. It's a major catastrophe.

64
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:11,140
OSHA, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration, moved immediately to investigate.

65
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:17,140
Still photos from the scene showed the stadium flag in full flourish,

66
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:20,820
an early sign that high winds had played the decisive role.

67
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:26,060
The wind was estimated at 10 to 20 miles per hour at ground level.

68
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:29,480
and even gustier where the load had dangled from Big Blue.

69
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:33,960
And since it's so close to the edge of the stadium,

70
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:38,620
you're getting a rolling effect from these winds and increasing your velocity.

71
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:42,140
And you're talking about this is like 190, 200 feet in the air

72
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:44,600
where the wind is coming over the stadium.

73
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:48,560
So it's coming against the stadium, rolling up over it, and blowing against the loads.

74
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:53,240
The heavier the load, the lower the acceptable wind speed.

75
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,700
But OSHA determined that crane operators, rushing to keep on schedule,

76
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:00,820
had negligently failed to take any wind measurements.

77
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:05,360
Winds over 10 miles per hour should have postponed the lift.

78
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:10,600
Instead, the crew went forward, with gusts exceeding 30 miles per hour.

79
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:19,460
The load had swayed so severely that a foot-thick kingpin bolt securing the crane's base had nearly sheared off,

80
00:06:19,461 --> 00:06:25,260
causing the loud pops heard by eyewitnesses and toppling the crane.

81
00:06:25,261 --> 00:06:26,680
Watch it, watch it!

82
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:40,960
When you're talking Big Blue, you're talking about a big team of people working on this thing.

83
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:45,640
You're talking about very sophisticated construction workers

84
00:06:45,641 --> 00:06:48,560
that are used to doing really heavy construction.

85
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:58,360
To see a crew of four separate operators agree to do that lift under those conditions amazed me.

86
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:00,180
It just shouldn't happen.

87
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:08,000
In 2000, a Milwaukee County jury found the crane operators 97% liable

88
00:07:08,001 --> 00:07:14,000
and the crane manufacturers 3% responsible for failing to fully communicate Big Blue's limitations.

89
00:07:14,001 --> 00:07:20,060
The jury awarded the families of the three dead steelworkers

90
00:07:20,061 --> 00:07:22,840
punitive damages of $94 million.

91
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:28,700
As a result of the accident, OSHA issued new directives

92
00:07:28,701 --> 00:07:30,820
governing the training of heavy construction workers.

93
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:34,200
Since then, there have been serious reform.

94
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,100
They're teaching the operators more engineering.

95
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:39,420
You have to check and you have to check and you have to check.

96
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:41,540
And your life happens on it.

97
00:07:41,541 --> 00:07:44,500
and your co-workers' lives depend on it.

98
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:50,860
At Miller Park, a statue of the three victims stands as a memorial to them

99
00:07:50,861 --> 00:07:55,360
and a warning to those who would succumb to the pressures of time and money.

100
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:59,820
Some people make a decision based on money rather than doing it safely.

101
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:06,440
I've done enough inspections in my career to see that happen several times.

102
00:08:06,441 --> 00:08:12,260
So taking a shortcut here to save a buck sometimes can cost a fortune.

103
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:20,520
In countries that severely limit workers' rights and their ability to seek damages,

104
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:24,440
there is an even greater temptation to place efficiency above safety.

105
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:30,640
China in the 21st century is an industrial powerhouse,

106
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:33,080
one of the world's fastest growing economies.

107
00:08:33,081 --> 00:08:40,640
But this economic giant still relies on an ancient source of power, coal, for 70% of its energy needs.

108
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:56,500
On February 16, 2005, a methane explosion tore through the Sunjiawan coal mine in northeastern China, killing 214 miners.

109
00:08:57,220 --> 00:08:59,740
For most victims, death was instantaneous.

110
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:01,760
You're like inside a bomb.

111
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:08,300
It's not like the movies where they see a ball of fire coming down the tunnel and say, uh-oh.

112
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:11,800
It happens in a millisecond.

113
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:14,200
It happens in a fraction of a fraction of a second.

114
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:20,680
Methane gas occurs naturally in the voids within rock and is released as a coal vein is torn open.

115
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:28,620
If the methane reaches concentrations in the air between 5 and 16 percent, the least little spark can ignite it.

116
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:35,460
That tiny explosion instantly combusts with floating coal dust, triggering a massive blast.

117
00:09:37,180 --> 00:09:43,320
The details of the Sunjiawan explosion are hidden behind the official secrecy of China's government-owned mines.

118
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:48,080
But that tragedy is just one in a long line of Chinese coal mine accidents.

119
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:58,500
Since 2002, explosions have killed 166 miners in the province of Sanji, 148 in Henan,

120
00:09:58,501 --> 00:10:01,640
and 124 in Heilongjiang.

121
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:07,140
Each year, an astonishing 6,000 Chinese miners perish

122
00:10:07,141 --> 00:10:09,480
in fires, floods, and explosions.

123
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:12,140
An average of 16 per day

124
00:10:12,141 --> 00:10:15,140
and 80% of the world's coal mining fatalities.

125
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:20,340
Chinese mines operate with antiquated technology

126
00:10:20,341 --> 00:10:22,460
dating back half a century and more.

127
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:26,080
The miners still use picks and shovels,

128
00:10:26,081 --> 00:10:29,840
So 20 men collect as much coal as one American miner.

129
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:36,060
This inefficiency means that Chinese mines are heavily populated with potential victims.

130
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:43,440
And the Chinese government has apparently made a cool and calculated cost-benefit analysis.

131
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:47,120
Coal miners are very badly paid in China.

132
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:50,260
They were very badly compensated for the deaths.

133
00:10:50,261 --> 00:10:55,680
If you add up more than 200 deaths in this coal mining accident alone,

134
00:10:56,140 --> 00:10:58,980
they were talking about paying off a total of $2 million.

135
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:06,300
If you're talking about that kind of penalty for having a very bad accident,

136
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:11,740
it's obviously going to be always cheaper to have the accident.

137
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:19,980
For decades, American miners toiled under conditions similar to those faced by Chinese miners today.

138
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:29,200
Poverty wages, no political power, and no government regulation made US mines deadly

139
00:11:29,201 --> 00:11:34,100
places to make a living.

140
00:11:34,101 --> 00:11:42,060
Then in 1969 came the formation of MSHA, the Mining Safety and Health Association.

141
00:11:42,061 --> 00:11:46,920
The new federal agency exercised powerful oversight, backed by inspections every three

142
00:11:46,921 --> 00:11:49,560
months, a practice that continues to this day.

143
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:55,620
The inspectors, they come quarterly, they do their inspections to make sure that we're

144
00:11:55,621 --> 00:11:56,660
doing what we're supposed to do.

145
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:58,520
And if we're not, they tell us about it.

146
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:02,380
And they have the right to shut you down if you're not doing it until you do do it right.

147
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:08,400
In American, as in Chinese coal mines, methane poses a grave hazard.

148
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:14,880
The January 2006 explosion at the Sago mine in West Virginia reminded the world of this

149
00:12:14,881 --> 00:12:15,900
ever-present danger.

150
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:23,100
But a multi-pronged approach to safety has drastically reduced methane accidents in the U.S.

151
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:28,800
Here at the National Coal Corporation in Jacksboro, Tennessee,

152
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:32,480
a high-pressure water system runs two miles underground,

153
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:35,840
providing enough water to constantly douch the coal dust.

154
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:39,340
So what you're looking at is the water sprays on the continuous miner.

155
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:41,840
It pulls the dust out of the air.

156
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:44,300
Therefore, it's a much better breathing environment

157
00:12:44,301 --> 00:12:46,400
and takes away the chances for explosion.

158
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:50,400
If it don't pull it down and get it out of the air, it becomes float dust.

159
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:55,120
And float dust is what causes the biggest explosion in a mine fire.

160
00:12:57,260 --> 00:13:02,060
Another defense against a buildup of coal dust is a robust ventilation system.

161
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:07,260
This giant fan produces 160,000 cubic feet of air per minute,

162
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:12,820
enough to create a steady stream of fresh air throughout the mine.

163
00:13:12,821 --> 00:13:19,180
methane content is constantly monitored all along the production line

164
00:13:19,181 --> 00:13:26,360
11 foot wide mechanical miners the machines that actually drill the rock

165
00:13:26,361 --> 00:13:29,680
have monitors that register methane levels at the rock face

166
00:13:29,681 --> 00:13:35,320
and unlike in china each section foreman carries his own methane detector

167
00:13:35,321 --> 00:13:40,760
you carry these with you at all times they got to be somebody on the section at all times with

168
00:13:40,761 --> 00:13:45,568
one of these. You have to check every place every 20 minutes.

169
00:13:45,569 --> 00:13:47,460
And you just turn it on,

170
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:52,560
you push this button right here, and you just go up and you sweep across the top.

171
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:58,720
And then you turn loose your button, and if you have any methane, it'll show up on this

172
00:13:58,721 --> 00:13:59,720
reading right here.

173
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:06,080
Both explosions and the sheer weight of the earth can trigger cave-ins.

174
00:14:06,081 --> 00:14:14,080
To maintain the mineshaft's structural integrity, four-foot-deep ceiling bolts are drilled every four feet.

175
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:23,820
Once a hole is made, long sticks of glue in plastic bags are inserted, followed by steel bolts that are spun into place for eight seconds.

176
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:30,140
The glue spreads into tiny fissures in the surrounding rock, creating an ultra-hard resin.

177
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:36,000
Together, the steel and the resin form a support structure as strong as a concrete beam.

178
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:44,100
As a final safeguard, American miners are required by law to carry a self-rescuer.

179
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:50,860
This is a CSE self-rescuer that all the men carry.

180
00:14:51,180 --> 00:14:53,520
It has an apparatus tube that goes in your mouth.

181
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:55,680
It also has goggles and a nose clip.

182
00:14:56,100 --> 00:14:57,160
There's a pin you pull.

183
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,860
You strap this thing to your chest after everything's been put on your face.

184
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:05,260
And it gives you one hour of oxygen so you can escape the mines.

185
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:13,080
Despite all the precautions, coal mining remains a very dangerous business.

186
00:15:13,081 --> 00:15:19,080
In recent years, new high-tech solutions have helped the search for miners trapped underground.

187
00:15:19,081 --> 00:15:26,080
Seismic location systems, sensitive enough to detect man-made vibrations.

188
00:15:26,081 --> 00:15:31,080
Remote-controlled robots, who can venture where humans cannot,

189
00:15:31,081 --> 00:15:34,580
and send back information via fiber optic cable

190
00:15:34,581 --> 00:15:40,000
and heat signature sensors that seek out the living.

191
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:47,120
But Chinese coal mines have yet to employ even 1970s-era technology,

192
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:49,080
much less more recent advances.

193
00:15:50,220 --> 00:15:55,140
In 2002, Beijing announced the country's first mine safety laws

194
00:15:55,141 --> 00:15:58,000
and promised nationwide inspection of coal mines.

195
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:02,300
But most observers feel that words have yet to be followed by deeds.

196
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:06,920
And the government keeps saying, well, it's going to do something about it.

197
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:10,480
It's going to set rules, going to set rules, going to set rules, going to set rules.

198
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:12,040
But nothing happens.

199
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:15,580
The problem remains, and people die.

200
00:16:19,160 --> 00:16:23,800
In 1942, the world's worst coal mining disaster on record

201
00:16:23,801 --> 00:16:28,820
killed 1,549 miners in Manchuria, northeastern China.

202
