choose Pacific Life, the power to help you succeed.

205
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:43,700
We now return to engineering disasters on Modern Marvels.

206
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:51,860
There's an old expression concerning how oil and water don't mix.

207
00:16:51,861 --> 00:16:59,740
On one winter afternoon in 1963, residents of Los Angeles found that old expression acquired new meaning.

208
00:17:00,780 --> 00:17:06,540
On that day, oil wells played a surprising part in an engineering failure involving oil exploration,

209
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:13,680
earthquake fault lines, and a giant public works project with a tiny hidden flaw.

210
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:21,380
A flaw that forced a crack in a reservoir to become a crevasse and unleash the fury of millions of gallons of water.

211
00:17:21,860 --> 00:17:26,640
And right here now, as we come up over the Baldwin Hills Reservoir, you can see the water.

212
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:34,400
It's unbelievable as this water and mud rushes down Cloverdale, engulfing everything in its path.

213
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:45,300
In 1951, when it was built in Los Angeles, the Baldwin Hills Dam was hailed as a $10 million state-of-the-art engineering masterwork.

214
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:51,380
It was a 19-acre reservoir that supplied drinking water for tens of thousands of residents.

215
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:59,980
The dam's hidden flaws became apparent at 3.38 p.m. on December 14, 1963.

216
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:04,660
We can see the main break in the reservoir itself.

217
00:18:04,661 --> 00:18:12,580
At that hour, the lining, a thin asphalt barrier that separated the bottom of the reservoir from the earth below, cracked apart.

218
00:18:13,340 --> 00:18:19,600
The designers relied far too much on the quarter-inch fiber-reinforced asphalt layer at the base of the liner system.

219
00:18:19,601 --> 00:18:30,620
You have to understand that it was assumed in the design, and it was absolutely essential to the stability of the reservoir, that no water get into the foundation.

220
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:40,549
Water in the foundation caused the bottom of the dam to erode away, and authorities were concerned that the compromised reservoir would flood a nearby residential

221
00:18:40,550 --> 00:18:41,250
neighborhood.

222
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:47,460
As police were called in to evacuate the area, word got out there was trouble at the dam.

223
00:18:47,461 --> 00:18:52,660
I remember it being a Saturday morning. I was in my room. I was 17 years old.

224
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:59,680
And I remember hearing a news report on the television in the living room that there was a problem with the Baldwin Hills Dam.

225
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:05,600
Richard Levine was a student photographer about to document his first big story.

226
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:10,600
Grabbing his camera and a few rolls of film, he raced to the scene.

227
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:15,280
When I got down there, it was absolutely amazing.

228
00:19:15,281 --> 00:19:17,960
It was the most chilling sight I'd ever seen.

229
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:21,060
I mean, to this day, I still think about it, and chills go down my spine.

230
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:25,280
To the horror of the city workers gathered at the site,

231
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:30,520
in about an hour, the rushing water eroded the dam's concrete abutment from below,

232
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:35,160
turning a pencil-thin crack into a 75-foot-wide gash.

233
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:42,160
When the abutment crumbled, 292 million gallons of water surged out of the reservoir.

234
00:19:42,161 --> 00:19:45,600
A large chunk of the inner cement here in the...

235
00:19:45,601 --> 00:19:50,060
This water was tumbling and rushing and boiling, and it was just washing homes out.

236
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:54,360
There's a picture. You can see those automobiles literally being pushed back.

237
00:19:54,760 --> 00:19:57,600
It reminded me of the Great Gorge of the Colorado River.

238
00:19:58,620 --> 00:20:02,000
And I shot until I eventually ran out of film.

239
00:20:12,160 --> 00:20:18,740
When it was over, 65 homes in the path of the flood were ripped apart.

240
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:26,700
Hundreds of other structures were damaged, and five people lay dead.

241
00:20:28,160 --> 00:20:32,940
I hadn't even seen the worst part, because I was up at the top of the dam when it washed out those homes.

242
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:34,860
They were just gone. It was just foundations left.

243
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:36,740
It just wiped them out.

244
00:20:36,741 --> 00:20:42,300
City workers drained the rest of the water from the dam

245
00:20:42,301 --> 00:20:46,240
And homeowners were left to sort through the wreckage

246
00:20:46,241 --> 00:20:53,480
Engineers went back to the original plans

247
00:20:53,481 --> 00:20:55,720
To learn what caused the lining to fail

248
00:20:55,721 --> 00:20:57,580
Fatally compromising the dam

249
00:20:57,581 --> 00:21:01,200
They discovered that the failure could be blamed

250
00:21:01,201 --> 00:21:03,260
Not only on how the dam was designed

251
00:21:03,261 --> 00:21:06,140
But also the location where it was built

252
00:21:06,141 --> 00:21:12,400
The Baldwin Hills Dam was constructed 400 feet from a large fault line.

253
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:16,500
Smaller secondary faults ran beneath the dam.

254
00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:22,520
If any of the faults were to shift, the lining was intended to flex with their movement.

255
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:26,580
In this way, the integrity of the dam would be maintained.

256
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:31,180
But that's not what happened on December 14th.

257
00:21:31,181 --> 00:21:36,180
This puzzled investigators because they knew the dam was built with the existing fault lines in mind.

258
00:21:37,180 --> 00:21:40,180
The designers attempted to deal with this.

259
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:45,180
They realized the faults were there, but they underestimated how much movement would occur across the faults.

260
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:48,180
The lining was made by putting down a layer of gravel,

261
00:21:50,180 --> 00:21:54,180
followed by a quarter-inch thick layer of asphalt reinforced with cotton fiber.

262
00:21:54,181 --> 00:21:55,034
cotton fiber.

263
00:21:55,035 --> 00:22:00,420
But when the faults moved, this material proved to be less flexible than designers

264
00:22:00,421 --> 00:22:02,343
may have wished, and it failed.

265
00:22:02,344 --> 00:22:06,500
Underestimating the potential for fault movement was a fatal error.

266
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:11,060
But what had caused the faults to move more than the designer expected?

267
00:22:13,740 --> 00:22:17,000
Investigators determined that other hidden factors were at play.

268
00:22:17,940 --> 00:22:24,260
This bowl, the Bowlin Hills Reservoir site, is on the eastern edge of the Inglewood oil field.

269
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:28,380
Inglewood oil field is probably about a square mile in area or more,

270
00:22:28,880 --> 00:22:32,880
and it has about over 600 wells at one time.

271
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:37,940
Pumping oil near the dam might have caused the land to become unstable

272
00:22:37,941 --> 00:22:40,160
and thus stimulate fault movement.

273
00:22:40,880 --> 00:22:46,120
But the oil companies were also using a method of oil extraction that raised a red flag.

274
00:22:46,121 --> 00:22:48,820
The method was called pressurized extraction.

275
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:54,220
The process is to eject water under high pressure

276
00:22:54,221 --> 00:23:00,120
and to cause that water and oil to push towards the active wells.

277
00:23:02,620 --> 00:23:06,900
The pressure extraction process involves pumping millions of gallons of water

278
00:23:06,901 --> 00:23:09,020
at high pressure beneath an oil field,

279
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:13,360
which forces the oil toward the surface, where it is easier to pump out.

280
00:23:13,361 --> 00:23:18,280
The extraction method apparently built up great below ground pressure.

281
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:28,020
I looked at the gauge on the well itself and it registered 600 pounds per square inch.

282
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:33,160
And that was on top of the ground and the well was either 1,000 or 2,000 feet deep.

283
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:37,360
So you can imagine the pressure that was being exerted on the ground

284
00:23:37,361 --> 00:23:41,120
which eventually caused the separation of the bottom here.

285
00:23:41,121 --> 00:23:48,620
Experts speculated that this intense pressure could have contributed to destabilizing the faults beneath the reservoir.

286
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:54,820
But in the end, authorities could agree on no single condition that would have caused the dam to fail.

287
00:23:56,600 --> 00:24:07,279
Rather, a combination of factors, fault movement, spurred on by oil exploration, and brittle structural elements in the lining of the dam, acted in concert to create

288
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:08,120
the disaster.

289
00:24:08,121 --> 00:24:20,557
Well, the fact that they didn't have an adequate review process during the design of the reservoir and its embankment led to a situation where design flaws were not

290
00:24:20,558 --> 00:24:21,258
captured.

291
00:24:23,060 --> 00:24:29,300
The Baldwin Hills Dam was never rebuilt, and after the reservoir was filled in, the land was turned into a park.

292
00:24:30,960 --> 00:24:34,220
Now only a serene hilltop marks the scene of the disaster.

293
00:24:34,221 --> 00:24:45,720
In part because of Baldwin Hills, dams built near fault lines now receive special scrutiny, and peer review is mandatory for all public works projects.

294
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:55,620
It's not uncommon to construct dams near earthquake faults, because the sloping topography conducive to building a dam is often formed by tectonic shifts.

295
00:24:56,260 --> 00:24:59,160
