298
00:25:07,940 --> 00:25:14,740
For 40 years, one machine was considered a nearly indispensable tool for buying shoes that fit.

299
00:25:15,780 --> 00:25:23,400
From the 1920s through the 1950s, no top-flight shoe store was complete without the wondrous shoe-fitting fluoroscope.

300
00:25:23,401 --> 00:25:31,100
Customers, most often children and their fast-growing feet, placed a foot above an x-ray tube.

301
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:37,180
The x-ray passed through the foot and shoe to project an image on a fluorescent screen.

302
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:43,460
There were three separate viewfinders, allowing junior, mom, and the salesman to share in the fun.

303
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:47,320
For the child, it was actually exciting.

304
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:50,600
You know, mom could say, come on, junior, let's go to the shoe store.

305
00:25:50,601 --> 00:25:52,040
and instead of the kid going,

306
00:25:52,180 --> 00:25:54,060
I have to go to the shoe store again?

307
00:25:54,480 --> 00:25:57,200
No, now they have this new piece of equipment.

308
00:25:57,360 --> 00:25:58,560
It's the most modern thing.

309
00:25:58,660 --> 00:26:03,140
You get to actually see your feet being measured for the proper size.

310
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:07,400
The fluoroscope enticed children into stores

311
00:26:07,401 --> 00:26:09,900
by making shopping a gee whiz experience.

312
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:13,700
And it reassured parents that they were doing right by their child.

313
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:20,260
In the 1920s, scientific child rearing was all the rage.

314
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:26,200
and mothers were being taught that they should use prepared formula instead of breast milk.

315
00:26:26,700 --> 00:26:32,000
Using perfectly scientifically fit shoes fit with this very, very well.

316
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:38,440
In 1950, there were 10,000 shoe-fitting fluoroscopes across America.

317
00:26:39,040 --> 00:26:45,480
But by 1960, the devices had all but vanished, unmasked as major health hazards.

318
00:26:45,481 --> 00:26:51,640
the machines emitted radiation doses of 20 to 75 rems per minute.

319
00:26:53,700 --> 00:27:01,140
In 2005, the maximum legal dose for workers in nuclear power plants was 5 rems per year.

320
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:11,980
In 1895, German scientist Wilhelm Konrad Rehnken discovered a new form of energy he named the X-ray.

321
00:27:11,981 --> 00:27:17,980
visible light cannot pass through most human tissue but x-rays which emanate

322
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:21,980
from radioactive substances operate on a wavelength that penetrates denser

323
00:27:21,981 --> 00:27:27,800
material including human skin even denser tissue like human bone will block

324
00:27:27,801 --> 00:27:34,300
the ray and cast a dark shadow on a photographic plate in the US Thomas

325
00:27:34,301 --> 00:27:38,200
Edison immediately saw the significance of the x-ray and put his lab to work

326
00:27:38,201 --> 00:27:40,600
developing its potential.

327
00:27:40,601 --> 00:27:45,620
The Edison team invented a new device, the fluoroscope, that allowed X-ray images to

328
00:27:45,621 --> 00:27:51,760
be imprinted on phosphorescent screens and viewed in real time.

329
00:27:51,761 --> 00:27:58,820
Edison lab assistant Clarence Daly paid a gruesome price for the advancing technology.

330
00:27:58,821 --> 00:28:04,360
Falling prey to radiation exposure, he lost both arms to malignant ulceration before dying

331
00:28:04,361 --> 00:28:06,540
an excruciating death.

332
00:28:06,541 --> 00:28:07,380
he was not alone.

333
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:10,400
In the first 20 years

334
00:28:10,401 --> 00:28:12,420
of the 20th century, dozens

335
00:28:12,421 --> 00:28:14,320
and dozens and dozens of

336
00:28:14,321 --> 00:28:16,380
workers who had worked

337
00:28:16,381 --> 00:28:18,120
with radiation of all kinds

338
00:28:18,121 --> 00:28:20,280
were dying. And in fact,

339
00:28:20,440 --> 00:28:22,540
they were glorified as martyrs

340
00:28:22,541 --> 00:28:23,260
to science.

341
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:26,100
Horrified by Daly's fate,

342
00:28:26,860 --> 00:28:28,400
Edison halted his radiological

343
00:28:28,401 --> 00:28:30,440
work and never again allowed

344
00:28:30,441 --> 00:28:31,620
himself to be x-rayed.

345
00:28:36,540 --> 00:28:45,840
But during World War I, a Boston doctor realized he could see more patients by using Edison's fluoroscope to examine veterans' feet without removing their boots.

346
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:49,200
The idea caught on.

347
00:28:49,880 --> 00:28:54,940
And by the 1920s, shoe-fitting fluoroscopes were a hugely successful sales gimmick.

348
00:28:55,440 --> 00:29:03,400
In the 1920s, the sales pitch was, it's good for your health because ill-fitting shoes can endanger your child's health.

349
00:29:03,401 --> 00:29:08,040
In the 1930s, with the Depression, the sales pitch changed.

350
00:29:08,460 --> 00:29:16,400
They started talking about, well, a pair of shoes that's well-fitting also will wear longer,

351
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:19,500
so you don't have to buy as many pairs of shoes.

352
00:29:20,300 --> 00:29:22,580
The advertising was extremely flexible.

353
00:29:22,960 --> 00:29:26,840
It fit the period the same way the shoes were supposed to fit the child.

354
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:33,260
Year after year, shoe salesmen irradiated their customers and themselves.

355
00:29:33,400 --> 00:29:34,620
on a regular basis.

356
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:37,240
When shoe store salesmen used these devices,

357
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:39,060
they weren't paying any attention to the dangers.

358
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:40,760
They'd move them around like a big piece of equipment,

359
00:29:40,900 --> 00:29:43,660
like you'd move a radio or move a television set.

360
00:29:44,120 --> 00:29:45,900
Careful, but not too careful.

361
00:29:46,580 --> 00:29:48,280
Things got jostled, things got damaged,

362
00:29:48,420 --> 00:29:49,360
shielding got shifted.

363
00:29:51,080 --> 00:29:53,340
So the foot that you're putting, or the feet,

364
00:29:53,420 --> 00:29:55,200
if you're young enough, that you're putting in there,

365
00:29:55,560 --> 00:29:57,060
not the only thing being irradiated.

366
00:29:57,061 --> 00:29:59,520
In fact, it's mom who's looking in one portal,

367
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:01,660
it's the salesman looking in the other portal,

368
00:30:01,661 --> 00:30:04,200
The child is up on the little podium looking in.

369
00:30:04,660 --> 00:30:06,480
All of them are being radiated all over.

370
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:12,600
Finally, in 1949, studies published in the New England Journal of Medicine

371
00:30:12,601 --> 00:30:14,660
confirmed what many had long suspected.

372
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:20,980
The machines produced enough radiation to have caused untold numbers of deformities,

373
00:30:21,440 --> 00:30:23,640
infertilities, and deadly cancers.

374
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:29,820
Here was a technological application that came with high risk

375
00:30:29,821 --> 00:30:31,960
and absolutely no reward.

376
00:30:34,060 --> 00:30:36,760
The shoe fluoroscope was a gimmick in a lot of ways.

377
00:30:37,380 --> 00:30:40,260
It is true you could get a picture of the foot inside the shoe

378
00:30:40,261 --> 00:30:41,760
that you probably couldn't get otherwise,

379
00:30:42,260 --> 00:30:43,900
but it really didn't matter.

380
00:30:44,700 --> 00:30:47,140
Good, careful measurements and careful fitting of a shoe

381
00:30:47,141 --> 00:30:48,900
by a skilled shoe store salesman

382
00:30:48,901 --> 00:30:51,060
would have given you the same result.

383
00:30:52,700 --> 00:30:55,540
Radiation pioneers who gave their lives to their work

384
00:30:55,541 --> 00:30:57,240
were called martyrs to science.

385
00:30:57,241 --> 00:31:02,620
Those who sacrificed their lives and their good health to the shoe-fitting fluoroscope

386
00:31:02,621 --> 00:31:05,940
might best be known as martyrs to commerce

387
00:31:05,941 --> 00:31:12,560
The radiation exposure for a typical customer using a shoe fluoroscope

388
00:31:12,561 --> 00:31:15,360
was 20 times that of a modern chest x-ray

