393
00:31:32,420 --> 00:31:36,380
Inland from Los Angeles, California, lies the Salton Basin,

394
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:41,440
a desert bowl of nearly 400 square miles, 280 feet below sea level.

395
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:44,980
Over the eons, the character of the basin has varied,

396
00:31:45,700 --> 00:31:50,560
sometimes dry and dusty, other times filled with rain or Colorado River water.

397
00:31:50,561 --> 00:31:56,800
Today the area contains a great sea that grows more toxic each year.

398
00:31:59,540 --> 00:32:05,720
Massive fish and bird die-offs have blighted its waters, and the implications for human health are grim.

399
00:32:10,920 --> 00:32:15,100
As the 20th century dawned, the basin was bone dry.

400
00:32:15,101 --> 00:32:22,340
But the privately owned Imperial Land Company was eager to develop towns and sell land.

401
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:29,080
In 1905, the company dug an irrigation canal to divert Colorado River water to surrounding farmland.

402
00:32:30,720 --> 00:32:33,220
But heavy flooding breached the canal banks.

403
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:37,640
And for 18 months, water flowed freely into the Salton Depression.

404
00:32:38,420 --> 00:32:44,560
Because of the flooding of the sea, there was a serious thought that it may not stop flooding.

405
00:32:44,561 --> 00:32:46,340
and it would rise and rise.

406
00:32:47,200 --> 00:32:49,960
Aggregate rock on railroad cars

407
00:32:49,961 --> 00:32:51,800
was collected throughout the United States,

408
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:55,900
brought to this area in order to stop the flooding.

409
00:32:57,380 --> 00:32:59,940
By the time the breach was finally repaired,

410
00:33:00,380 --> 00:33:02,280
a vast inland oasis had formed.

411
00:33:02,900 --> 00:33:05,020
The present day, Salton Sea.

412
00:33:06,640 --> 00:33:09,060
Everyone thought at the time

413
00:33:09,061 --> 00:33:11,780
that once they fixed the breach,

414
00:33:11,781 --> 00:33:15,420
the bowl would simply dry up again, would evaporate.

415
00:33:15,580 --> 00:33:16,280
Didn't happen.

416
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:22,040
Engineers had failed to account for the growing inflow of agricultural wastewater.

417
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:30,540
In 1924, the federal government designated the Salton Sea a permanent farm drainage reservoir.

418
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:34,900
California's Imperial Valley would help feed the world,

419
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:38,100
and its watery residue would maintain the sea.

420
00:33:38,101 --> 00:33:47,220
After World War II, you have a bunch of things happen that are all trends in the wrong direction.

421
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:49,800
First of all, more agriculture.

422
00:33:50,580 --> 00:33:52,080
More water use.

423
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:54,860
More agricultural chemicals being used.

424
00:33:55,360 --> 00:33:58,060
They go way beyond what's natural.

425
00:33:58,061 --> 00:33:59,300
You have DDT.

426
00:33:59,600 --> 00:34:01,060
You have defoliants.

427
00:34:01,760 --> 00:34:05,060
You have other persistent pesticides.

428
00:34:05,061 --> 00:34:09,960
It's pesticides that, once they're let loose in nature, they don't go away.

429
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:11,280
They just exist.

430
00:34:12,720 --> 00:34:15,340
But the sea still looked fresh and inviting.

431
00:34:16,460 --> 00:34:19,420
In the 1950s, it became a major resort center.

432
00:34:20,220 --> 00:34:23,980
As Southern California's booming population flocked to buy seaside houses,

433
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:27,780
joined the beach and yacht club, swim, fish, and boat.

434
00:34:30,200 --> 00:34:34,940
For decades, Salton Sea State Park was one of the most popular destinations in America.

435
00:34:35,060 --> 00:34:41,780
In the heyday of the resort area, this was a popular recreation area for the Hollywood elite,

436
00:34:42,140 --> 00:34:45,840
for a lot of Los Angeles and San Diego Basin to come over and recreate.

437
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:50,540
There were boat races, there's camping facilities, and it's a big open body of water.

438
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:58,540
Today, the area is all but abandoned, littered with reminders of its bright past.

439
00:34:58,541 --> 00:35:05,220
it has fallen prey to a ticking time bomb

440
00:35:05,221 --> 00:35:11,160
as each year agricultural runoff deposited 5 million tons of salt into the sea

441
00:35:11,161 --> 00:35:15,600
people did recognize 30 or 40 years ago

442
00:35:15,601 --> 00:35:19,760
that the future of the Salton Sea was bleak if nothing was done

443
00:35:19,761 --> 00:35:24,680
you keep putting water that's laden with salt into a place where the salt can't escape

444
00:35:24,681 --> 00:35:26,540
and you're evaporating off the water

445
00:35:26,541 --> 00:35:30,460
It doesn't take a mental giant to realize you're increasing the salt content.

446
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:34,000
And over time, that's going to kill the biology.

447
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:40,600
By 1995, salt levels had reached 45 parts per thousand,

448
00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:44,380
25% saltier than the Pacific Ocean.

449
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:51,340
As its ecology grew unstable,

450
00:35:51,640 --> 00:35:55,660
the area was bedeviled by a series of massive bird and fish die-offs.

451
00:35:56,540 --> 00:36:03,540
In 1996, Type C avian botulism killed thousands of white and brown pelicans.

452
00:36:03,541 --> 00:36:11,540
The exact cause of the outbreak remains unknown, although it was apparently not connected to the water's rising salinity.

453
00:36:11,541 --> 00:36:18,540
The Salton Sea is a highly complex ecosystem, and it still supports hundreds of species.

454
00:36:18,541 --> 00:36:22,580
hundreds of species.

455
00:36:22,581 --> 00:36:27,660
As other wetlands deteriorate or disappear, birds from near and far rely on the sea for

456
00:36:27,661 --> 00:36:29,500
their very life.

457
00:36:29,501 --> 00:36:33,500
We used to have wetlands up and down the coast of California, but we've lost more than 90%

458
00:36:33,501 --> 00:36:39,500
of them, and so many of the 400-plus bird species at the Salton Sea use this as their

459
00:36:39,501 --> 00:36:43,220
sole refueling stop in Southern California.

460
00:36:43,221 --> 00:36:47,340
Despite the challenges for the Salton Sea, it's one of the most important ecosystems

461
00:36:47,341 --> 00:36:50,400
in the United States for birds and other wildlife.

462
00:36:50,401 --> 00:36:53,640
It has over 400 bird species over the course of the year.

463
00:36:53,641 --> 00:36:56,040
That's two-thirds of all the bird species

464
00:36:56,041 --> 00:36:58,580
in the United States and Canada.

465
00:36:58,581 --> 00:37:03,400
It truly is an oasis in the desert for wildlife.

466
00:37:03,401 --> 00:37:07,740
But it's no longer an oasis for humans.

467
00:37:07,741 --> 00:37:10,860
Vacationers and nature lovers have all but vanished,

468
00:37:10,861 --> 00:37:14,100
driven away by serious health hazards.

469
00:37:14,101 --> 00:37:18,040
Decaying fish, algae, and bacteria emit hydrogen sulfide gas,

470
00:37:18,640 --> 00:37:20,760
ripe with the pungent odor of rotting eggs.

471
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:24,820
Even more disturbing, increasing amounts of Imperial Valley water

472
00:37:24,821 --> 00:37:27,100
is now diverted to urban Southern California,

473
00:37:28,020 --> 00:37:31,960
reducing inflows to the Salton Sea and causing it to shrink.

474
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:36,700
As the sea becomes smaller, the soil and sediment that it leaves behind,

475
00:37:36,820 --> 00:37:39,880
which is now exposed to the air, is very light.

476
00:37:39,881 --> 00:37:44,440
It's very light particulate matter, and with not very much wind at all,

477
00:37:44,740 --> 00:37:47,800
it can create enormous and very dangerous dust storms

478
00:37:47,801 --> 00:37:51,960
that cause a whole range of respiratory illnesses, some quite long term.

479
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:56,260
As the sea becomes smaller, the remaining exposed basin

480
00:37:56,261 --> 00:38:00,200
could become the single largest source of dust pollution in the United States.

481
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:04,780
If current trends continue, the Salton Sea will die.

482
00:38:04,781 --> 00:38:11,180
But there are plans that could once again make it a haven for all forms of life.

483
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:18,160
The leading restoration plans look to divide the sea with a giant barrier,

484
00:38:18,860 --> 00:38:22,540
concentrating excess salt in a so-called brine sink.

485
00:38:23,460 --> 00:38:25,680
It's very ambitious. It's very expensive.

486
00:38:26,080 --> 00:38:28,700
The barrier, depending on where you place it,

487
00:38:29,740 --> 00:38:32,560
is going to be one of the longest earthen barriers in the world.

488
00:38:33,240 --> 00:38:37,100
Restoration plans would segregate bodies of water on each end of the sea,

489
00:38:37,620 --> 00:38:41,340
allowing water and salt to flow from them into the central brine sink area.

490
00:38:42,420 --> 00:38:47,080
In the shallow southern end, a series of dikes would cordon off a wildlife refuge.

491
00:38:47,840 --> 00:38:51,860
In the north, a seven-mile-long, 50-foot-deep barrier,

492
00:38:52,400 --> 00:38:55,040
requiring 60 million cubic yards of rock fill,

493
00:38:55,560 --> 00:38:59,320
or 20 times the amount of material used to build the Great Pyramid in Egypt,

494
00:38:59,740 --> 00:39:02,060
would create a lake for human recreation.

495
00:39:02,560 --> 00:39:10,920
While the costs and complications of restoring part of the Salton Sea are enormous, the price

496
00:39:10,921 --> 00:39:15,680
of doing nothing may be higher still.

497
00:39:15,681 --> 00:39:21,100
Into the 1980s, Salton Sea State Park was so popular that it attracted more visitors

498
00:39:21,101 --> 00:39:23,880
each year than Yosemite National Park.

